window.docContents[50].resolve({"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Forward-Reasoning-with--apply":{"contents":"\n\n\n\nThe ordinary apply tactic is a form of \"backward\nreasoning.\" It says \"We are trying to prove a and we know\nb → a, so if we can prove b we'll be done.\"By contrast, the variant apply ... at ... is \"forward reasoning\":\nit says \"We know b and we know b → a, so we also know a.\"\n\nexample (n m p q : Nat)\n    (h : n = m → p = q)\n    (hnm : n = m) :\n    p = q := by\n  apply h at hnm\n  exact hnm\n\n\n\n\n\n\nYou can apply tactics in multiple places at the same time, including the goal:\n\nexample (n m : Nat) (h : n + 0 = m) : n = m + 0 := by\n  rw [Nat.add_zero] at h ⊢\n  assumption\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics","header":"7.4. Forward Reasoning with  apply","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Forward-Reasoning-with--apply"}});